Rem ==================================================================
Rem seedvt100test.shoddy — test program for seedvt100.shoddy and
Rem seedkeys.shoddy
Rem
Rem Assert-driven, in the cuttletest / reckonertest / seedtest pattern:
Rem it prints what it is doing and stops the run at the first assertion
Rem that fails, ending with ALL ASSERTIONS PASSED when it does not.
Rem
Rem TWO SEEDS IN ONE SUITE, because they were one mistake. Both machines
Rem were listed in halifax-core.shoddy as unreachable "live resources" —
Rem vt100 as "the terminal, which this shell already owns", keys as "raw
Rem keystrokes, which InputLine already owns". Neither touches either.
Rem vt100 builds escape-sequence STRINGS and its own header says
Rem "nothing here writes to the screen"; keys folds a NUMBER into a key
Rem name and includes nothing at all. The suite that proves a machine is
Rem pure is the one that seeds it and gets answers back, so both are
Rem proved together here.
Rem
Rem WHAT IS ACTUALLY WORTH ASSERTING for a seed over pure functions is
Rem not that every one of ninety-odd constants is bridged — the
Rem registration is table-driven, so they are bridged or none of them
Rem is. It is that the TABLE agrees with the machine, that the words
Rem which take numbers guard them, and that the two directions of the
Rem VT100 protocol meet: a sequence this seed builds is a sequence its
Rem own VTEVALKEY reads back.
Rem
Rem Fixtures: none. Nothing here opens anything.
Rem
Rem Run: bin\mill run tst\seedvt100test.shoddy      (from the repo root)
Rem ==================================================================

Include "../machines/cuttle.shoddy"
Include "../machines/reckoner.shoddy"
Include "../machines/seq.shoddy"
Include "../machines/str.shoddy"
Rem The machine itself, so the assertions can compare each bridged word
Rem against the Def it was written from rather than against a second
Rem copy of the escape sequence typed out here.
Include "../machines/vt100.shoddy"
Include "../machines/seeds/seedvt100.shoddy"
Include "../machines/seeds/seedkeys.shoddy"

Rem ---- reading an answer ------------------------------------------------

Def Ran(st As RckState, line As String) As RckState
    Select Case RckEval(st, line)
        Case RckNext(st2, out)
            st2
        Case RckStop(st2, why, out)
            st2
        Case Else
            st

Def Bad(st As RckState, line As String) As Boolean
    Select Case RckEval(st, line)
        Case RckStop(st2, why, out)
            True
        Case Else
            False

Def Why(st As RckState, line As String) As String
    Select Case RckEval(st, line)
        Case RckStop(st2, why, out)
            why
        Case Else
            ""

Def Text(st As RckState) As String
    Join(Map(CutCells(RckStack(st)), Fn(c) => CutShow(c, RckFmt(st))), " | ")

Def Q(s As String) As String
    Chr(34) & s & Chr(34)

Def Refuses(st As RckState, line As String, why As String) As Boolean
    Bad(st, line) And Instr(Why(st, line), why) > 0 And Text(Ran(st, line)) = Text(st) And Text(Ran(Ran(st, line), "1 1 +")) = Text(Ran(st, "1 1 +"))

Rem The top of the stack as a plain string, so an escape sequence can be
Rem compared against what the machine itself builds without CutShow's
Rem quoting getting in the way.
Def TopStr(st As RckState) As String
    If CutDepth(RckStack(st)) < 1 Then
        "<empty>"
    Else
        StrOf(First(CutCells(RckStack(st))))

Def StrOf(c As Cell) As String
    Select Case c
        Case CutStr(s)
            s
        Case Else
            "<not a string>"

Def Main()
    Let s0 = RckSeedKeys(RckSeedVt100(RckNew()))

    Print("--- both seeds are in the dictionary, grouped and documented ---")
    Assert(RckKnows(s0, "VTCLEARSCREEN") And RckKnows(s0, "VTCURSORPOS") And RckKnows(s0, "VTEVALKEY"), "THE VT100 WORDS ARE THERE")
    Assert(RckKnows(s0, "KEYNAME") And RckKnows(s0, "KEYGLYPH") And RckKnows(s0, "KEYIS"), "AND SO ARE THE KEYS WORDS")
    Assert(Contains(RckWords(s0), "-- seed-vt100 --"), "VT100 HAS A GROUP OF ITS OWN")
    Assert(Contains(RckWords(s0), "-- seed-keys --"), "AND SO DOES KEYS")
    Assert(All(RckDict(s0), Fn(e) => WordEffect(e) <> "" And WordDesc(e) <> ""), "NO WORD IS WITHOUT AN EFFECT LINE AND A DESCRIPTION")
    Assert(All(RckDict(s0), Fn(e) => Length(RckHelp(s0, WordName(e))) = 3), "AND HELP ANSWERS FOR EVERY ONE OF THEM")
    Rem NOTHING HERE TOUCHES THE WORLD, and that is the whole claim these
    Rem two seeds rest on. Every word is RckPure: a machine that only
    Rem builds strings cannot be doing anything else.
    Assert(All(RckDict(s0), Fn(e) => Holds(RckHelp(s0, WordName(e)), "touches nothing")), "AND EVERY WORD IN BOTH SEEDS TOUCHES NOTHING, WHICH IS THE POINT")

    Print("--- the constant table agrees with the machine it was written from ---")
    Rem The registration is table-driven, so a wrong entry is the one
    Rem failure mode: a name that answers some OTHER sequence. Each of
    Rem these compares the word's answer against the machine's own Def.
    Assert(TopStr(Ran(s0, "VTCLEARSCREEN")) = ClearScreen(), "VTCLEARSCREEN IS CLEARSCREEN")
    Assert(TopStr(Ran(s0, "VTBOLD")) = Bold(), "VTBOLD IS BOLD")
    Assert(TopStr(Ran(s0, "VTATTRSOFF")) = AttrsOff(), "VTATTRSOFF IS ATTRSOFF")
    Assert(TopStr(Ran(s0, "VTCLEAREOL")) = ClearEol(), "VTCLEAREOL IS CLEAREOL")
    Assert(TopStr(Ran(s0, "VTESC")) = Esc(), "VTESC IS THE BARE ESCAPE")
    Assert(TopStr(Ran(s0, "VTCSI")) = Csi(), "AND VTCSI IS ESCAPE AND BRACKET")
    Assert(TopStr(Ran(s0, "VTVT52HOME")) = Vt52CursorHome(), "AND THE VT52 HALF IS BRIDGED TOO")
    Assert(TopStr(Ran(s0, "VTLED4ON")) = Led4On(), "AND THE KEYBOARD LEDS, WHICH ARE THE FAR END OF THE TABLE")
    Rem No two words may answer the same sequence under different names,
    Rem which is what a copy-paste slip in the table would look like.
    Assert(TopStr(Ran(s0, "VTBOLD")) <> TopStr(Ran(s0, "VTBLINK")), "AND TWO DIFFERENT WORDS ARE TWO DIFFERENT SEQUENCES")

    Print("--- a session joins them the way a program would ---")
    Rem The core + joins two STRINGs, so a whole screenful of setup is
    Rem one expression. This is what the seed is FOR.
    Assert(TopStr(Ran(s0, "VTCLEARSCREEN VTBOLD + " & Q("HELLO") & " +")) = ClearScreen() & Bold() & "HELLO", "THREE PIECES JOIN INTO THE STRING A PROGRAM WOULD HAVE BUILT")

    Print("--- the words that take numbers guard them ---")
    Assert(TopStr(Ran(s0, "3 5 VTCURSORPOS")) = CursorPos(3, 5), "VTCURSORPOS IS CURSORPOS")
    Assert(TopStr(Ran(s0, "2 VTCURSORUP")) = CursorUp(2), "AND VTCURSORUP IS CURSORUP")
    Assert(TopStr(Ran(s0, "1 10 VTSCROLLREGION")) = SetScrollRegion(1, 10), "AND VTSCROLLREGION IS SETSCROLLREGION")
    Let g = Ran(s0, "1 2")
    Rem A VT100 counts from 1, and a 0 in the sequence is accepted by the
    Rem terminal and does something other than what was meant — so it is
    Rem refused here rather than built.
    Assert(Refuses(g, "0 5 VTCURSORPOS", "ROWS AND COLUMNS COUNT FROM 1"), "ROW ZERO, WHICH THE TERMINAL WOULD ACT ON")
    Assert(Refuses(g, "3 0 VTCURSORPOS", "ROWS AND COLUMNS COUNT FROM 1"), "AND COLUMN ZERO")
    Assert(Refuses(g, "0 VTCURSORUP", "THE COUNT MUST BE 1 OR MORE"), "AND A MOVE OF NOTHING")
    Assert(Refuses(g, "-2 VTCURSORDOWN", "VTCURSORDOWN"), "AND A NEGATIVE MOVE, WHICH RCKWHOLEARG REFUSES FIRST")
    Assert(Refuses(g, "1.5 VTCURSORLEFT", "VTCURSORLEFT"), "AND A FRACTIONAL ONE")
    Assert(Refuses(g, Q("x") & " 5 VTCURSORPOS", "VTCURSORPOS"), "AND A ROW THAT IS NOT A NUMBER AT ALL")
    Rem The scrolling region is the one pair that is a RANGE rather than
    Rem a point, so it has an ordering to get wrong.
    Assert(Refuses(g, "10 1 VTSCROLLREGION", "THE TOP LINE MUST BE ABOVE THE BOTTOM ONE"), "A SCROLLING REGION UPSIDE DOWN")
    Assert(Refuses(g, "5 5 VTSCROLLREGION", "THE TOP LINE MUST BE ABOVE THE BOTTOM ONE"), "AND ONE OF NO HEIGHT AT ALL")

    Print("--- the two directions of the protocol meet ---")
    Rem What this seed BUILDS, its own VTEVALKEY READS. That is the
    Rem assertion that the bridge is a bridge and not two halves.
    Assert(Text(Ran(s0, "VTESC " & Q("OP") & " + VTEVALKEY")) = Q("pf1"), "A SEQUENCE BUILT FROM VTESC IS READ BACK AS PF1")
    Assert(Text(Ran(s0, "VTESC " & Q("A") & " + VTEVALKEY")) = Q("up"), "AND THE NORMAL-MODE ARROW AS UP")
    Assert(Text(Ran(s0, "VTESC " & Q("OA") & " + VTEVALKEY")) = Q("up"), "AND THE APPLICATION-MODE ONE AS UP TOO, WHICH IS THE WHOLE POINT OF EVALKEY")
    Assert(Text(Ran(s0, "VTESC " & Q("OD") & " + VTEVALKEY")) = Q("left"), "AND LEFT")
    Assert(Text(Ran(s0, Q("5") & " VTEVALKEY")) = Q("5"), "A KEYPAD DIGIT ANSWERS ITSELF")
    Assert(Text(Ran(s0, Q("not a sequence") & " VTEVALKEY")) = Q("unknown"), "AND ANYTHING UNRECOGNISED IS UNKNOWN RATHER THAN A REFUSAL")
    Assert(Refuses(g, "5 VTEVALKEY", "VTEVALKEY"), "BUT A NUMBER IS NOT A RAW SEQUENCE AND IS REFUSED")

    Print("--- keys: a code, named ---")
    Assert(Text(Ran(s0, "262 KEYNAME")) = Q("left"), "262 IS THE LEFT ARROW")
    Assert(Text(Ran(s0, "265 KEYNAME")) = Q("down"), "AND 265 THE DOWN ARROW")
    Assert(Text(Ran(s0, "32 KEYNAME")) = Q("space"), "32 IS THE SPACE BAR")
    Assert(Text(Ran(s0, "13 KEYNAME")) = Q("enter"), "13 IS ENTER")
    Assert(Text(Ran(s0, "27 KEYNAME")) = Q("esc"), "27 IS ESC")
    Rem A letter or digit answers its own glyph rather than "char",
    Rem because "char" is an answer that makes the caller ask again.
    Assert(Text(Ran(s0, "81 KEYNAME")) = Q("Q"), "AND A LETTER KEY ANSWERS ITS OWN GLYPH")
    Assert(Text(Ran(s0, "55 KEYNAME")) = Q("7"), "AS DOES A DIGIT")
    Assert(Text(Ran(s0, "9999 KEYNAME")) = Q("other"), "AND A CODE THE TABLE DOES NOT KNOW IS OTHER, WHICH IS AN ANSWER AND NOT A FAILURE")
    Assert(Text(Ran(s0, "81 KEYGLYPH")) = Q("Q"), "KEYGLYPH ANSWERS THE GLYPH")
    Assert(Text(Ran(s0, "262 KEYGLYPH")) = Q(""), "AND EMPTY FOR A NAMED KEY, WHICH IS WHERE KEYNAME EARNS ITS KEEP")
    Rem Physical letter keys always report uppercase, so the comparison
    Rem is deliberately case-insensitive.
    Assert(Text(Ran(s0, "81 " & Q("q") & " KEYIS")) = "True", "KEYIS MATCHES A LOWERCASE LETTER AGAINST THE PHYSICAL KEY")
    Assert(Text(Ran(s0, "81 " & Q("Q") & " KEYIS")) = "True", "AND AN UPPERCASE ONE")
    Assert(Text(Ran(s0, "81 " & Q("z") & " KEYIS")) = "False", "AND SAYS SO WHEN IT IS A DIFFERENT KEY")
    Assert(Refuses(g, "-1 KEYNAME", "KEYNAME"), "A NEGATIVE KEY CODE IS REFUSED RATHER THAN CALLED OTHER")
    Assert(Refuses(g, "1.5 KEYNAME", "KEYNAME"), "AND A FRACTIONAL ONE")
    Assert(Refuses(g, Q("left") & " KEYNAME", "KEYNAME"), "AND A NAME, WHICH IS THE ANSWER RATHER THAN THE QUESTION")

    Print("--- and the session is exactly as it was ---")
    Rem Nothing in either seed opens, writes or reads anything, so there
    Rem is nothing to clean up and nothing to have leaked.
    Assert(IsEmpty(RckBoundNames(Ran(s0, "VTCLEARSCREEN VTCURSORHOME + 262 KEYNAME"))), "NOTHING EITHER SEED DOES BINDS A RESOURCE")
    Assert(Text(Ran(g, "1 1 +")) = "2 | 2 | 1", "AND THE STACK IS STILL THERE AND STILL EVALUATING")

    Print("")
    Print("ALL ASSERTIONS PASSED")

Def Holds(lines As List Of String, sub As String) As Boolean
    Any(lines, Fn(l) => Instr(l, sub) > 0)
